  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Atascosa’s name means “boggy” in Spanish, a nod to the marshy lands that early settlers first crossed after the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atascosa%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s post office opened in 1872, marking the first official federal presence in this rural community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atascosa%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1900, Atascosa was already mining coal and producing pottery, two industries that helped sustain its small population.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atascosa%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the mid‑1930s, a church, a lodge, and several stores served about 300 residents, giving the town a modest but tight‑knit community feel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atascosa%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Geographically, Atascosa sits 14 miles southwest of downtown San Antonio, nestled between Von Ormy and Lytle along Old Pearsall Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atascosa%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today, Atascosa is part of the San Antonio Metropolitan Statistical Area, linking it to one of Texas’s largest urban centers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
